<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  PlayNexa worker — per-user LaunchAgent.

  A LaunchAgent, not a LaunchDaemon. A daemon runs as root before login and would have no Keychain
  of its own to read; an agent runs as the worker account, which is the account the Keychain item is
  bound to. The worker needs no privilege beyond that account.

  THERE IS NO SECRET IN THIS FILE. A plist in ~/Library/LaunchAgents is readable by every process
  the user runs and is copied into Time Machine backups. WORKER_ID and the URL are not secrets;
  the credential is read from the Keychain at startup. See src/worker-client/keychain.js.

  KeepAlive is conditional, not unconditional. `SuccessfulExit false` restarts the worker when it
  exits non-zero — a crash, a lost database, a bad deploy — and LEAVES IT STOPPED when it exits 0,
  which is what a deliberate shutdown does. Unconditional KeepAlive would fight `workerctl stop`.

  ThrottleInterval is 60 rather than the default 10. A worker that cannot read its credential exits
  2 immediately, and at the default this would respawn six times a minute for ever, filling the log
  with the same line and making the real problem harder to see.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>ai.playnexa.worker</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>__NODE_BIN__</string>
    <string>__WORKER_DIR__/src/worker-client/run.js</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>__WORKER_DIR__</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>WORKER_API_BASE_URL</key>
    <string>__API_BASE_URL__</string>
    <key>WORKER_ID</key>
    <string>__WORKER_ID__</string>
    <!--
      NODE_ENV and PLAYNEXA_WORKER_ENV answer different questions, and conflating them is how a
      development worker ends up with production's behaviour or production with development's.

      NODE_ENV=production means "run optimized" — it is what node and the libraries read. It stays
      `production` on a development worker ON PURPOSE: flipping it would change library behaviour
      and the development worker would stop resembling the thing being certified.

      PLAYNEXA_WORKER_ENV is this repo's own label, and nothing infers it. `workerctl install`
      writes `production` unless given `--env development`, so an unstated environment is treated
      as production and every development-only path stays shut.

      It is deliberately NOT derived from the API URL. The development API's hostname is
      `m7-dev-worker-api-production.up.railway.app` — it contains the word "production". A guard
      that read the environment out of that string would be wrong in the dangerous direction.
    -->
    <key>NODE_ENV</key>
    <string>production</string>
    <key>PLAYNEXA_WORKER_ENV</key>
    <string>__WORKER_ENV__</string>
__ENV_EXTRA__
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>

  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>60</integer>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>

  <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
  <true/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_DIR__/worker.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_DIR__/worker.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
